  "id": "ms-industrial-opcua-telemetry-v1",
  "title": "Industrial telemetry from OPC UA equipment to analytics",
  "subtitle": "An edge-connected industrial pattern collects OPC UA telemetry, processes it near equipment, and routes selected data to cloud analytics. Synthetic Azure reference; no deployment is implied.",
  "description": "### Problem\nFactories need interoperable collection and analysis of equipment telemetry across sites, including local edge processing and cloud insight workflows.\n\n### Flow and Azure mapping\nIndustrial controllers and sensors expose OPC UA data to an edge environment running Azure IoT Operations on Azure Arc-enabled Kubernetes. Edge data flows normalize or filter telemetry, then deliver selected records through an Azure Event Hubs-compatible ingestion path to Azure Data Explorer or a Fabric eventhouse for query and dashboards. A distinct optional branch can send events to alerting or maintenance workflows.\n\n### Trade-offs and assumptions\nThe source solution uses a specific demonstration and includes Azure IoT Operations, PostgreSQL, and analytics options; this catalog diagram is a smaller conceptual abstraction, not a deployment reproduction. Site connectivity, OPC UA certificate trust, buffering, schema governance, and industrial safety remain design requirements. Do not depict cloud analytics as a replacement for deterministic control loops.\n\nSource: [Microsoft Learn](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/guide/iot/iot-industrial-solution-architecture).\n\n## Source provenance\n\n- Origin provider: Microsoft\n- Target: Azure conceptual adaptation\n- Primary source: [OPC UA reference solution](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/guide/iot/iot-industrial-solution-architecture)\n- Reviewed: 2026-11-06\n\n## Supplementary context\n\n- [Get started with IoT architecture design](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/guide/iot/iot-get-started) — Reviewed: 2026-11-06; Supplementary provider context; not a copied diagram or implementation.\n\n## Catalog status\n\nThis is an original, editable Azure conceptual adaptation. It is synthetic reference material, not a deployed system, certified vendor architecture, or automatic deployment plan.\n\n## Mapping caveats\n\n- The source cautions that the sample is not a supported product offering and should be validated before production use.",
